WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.560
So welcome back, everyone.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.200
So on Monday, Tatsu
did an excellent job

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.440
of describing a high
level overview of GPUs

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.640
and how to think about
performance and all the quirks

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.425
that come with GPUs.

00:00:19.425 --> 00:00:21.800
This lecture is going to be
a continuation of that, where

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.040
we're going to dive more
deeply into the code,

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.480
write some Triton kernels, and
as well as do some benchmarking

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.520
and profiling.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.880
So just to start,
just to refresh

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.960
what's going on
with GPUs, here is

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:44.520
a simplified diagram of what
a typical GPU looks like.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.960
There's memory, and then
the actual GPU chip.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.400
And just to keep in
your head what are

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.200
the characteristics of a GPU.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.820
So of course, we're
talking about NVIDIA GPUs,

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.090
but Tatsu also talked about
CPUs, and there's AMD and so on.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.510
But focusing on NVIDIA GPUs.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:11.570
We've had multiple generations,
from A100s, to H100s, to B200s.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.390
And in each generation,
you have a GPU.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:21.170
It has a number of Streaming
Multiprocessors, or SMs.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:27.070
And the number of SMs is about
100, between 100 and 200.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.250
So that hasn't really
changed that much.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:38.170
And then within the individual
SM, there's a set of registers.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:49.170
So B200s have 65,000 registers
for a total of 256K per SM.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.330
And this also hasn't
changed that much.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:57.150
In addition, there's a L1
cache as well as shared memory.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.810
So remember, these
are the same memory,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.060
shared memory you can control.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.910
L1, you can't.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.110
This is per SM.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.710
And this is in the same
order ballpark of size.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.110
And then there's L2 cache.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.030
And this is not per SM,
this is for the whole chip.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:17.870
And this is a bit larger.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.870
And then finally, you have a
High Bandwidth Memory, or HBM,

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:22.800
which is large.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.550
And you see that this
is the number that's

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.670
actually going up quite a bit.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:28.990
OK.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.270
And then in addition
to size, you

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.830
can think about what's
happening with the bandwidth.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:40.450
And essentially, it's
inversely correlated.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.870
So registers are very fast,
L1 is slightly less fast,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.850
L2 is less fast, and high
bandwidth memory is the slowest.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.070
Although eight
terabytes a second

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:59.960
is still not that slow in
the grand scheme of things.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.880
So this is the main hierarchy
you should have in your head.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.560
Large memory is slow
and far but big.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.820
And fast memory like registers
and L1 resides on the SM.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.520
It's local and it's
fast, but small.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.600
OK, that's the environment
we're dealing with.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.940
OK, so then, how do
you program a GPU?

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.040
So the program
model is as follows.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.680
So there's notions
of threads, which

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.760
each thread executes
a piece of code

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.780
on a small part of the data.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.760
So each one of these arrows, you
can think about it as a thread.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.720
These threads are organized
into thread blocks,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.680
also known as Concurrent
Thread Arrays, or CTAs.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.300
And this is a group of threads.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.160
And then finally, you have a
collection of thread blocks

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.760
which forms the grid.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.680
So when you launch a
kernel, you're basically

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.090
launching a grid of threads and
thread blocks to all parallel

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:59.690
simultaneously do
some computation.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.350
OK, so this is simplified.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.590
There's some other things
that are happening.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.530
For example, there's
H100s and B200s also

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.090
have thread block
clusters, which

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.650
are clusters of
thread blocks that

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.370
enable some amount of
distribution memory.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.550
Also, B200s have tensor memory.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.329
Now for tensor cores, which
is somewhere between registers

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:22.650
and shared memory.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.030
Some of these things are
invisible to the programmer,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.370
but they are in the hardware.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:34.490
But we won't worry about
them for this intro lecture.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.490
OK, so one thing
you might ask about

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.450
is, why do we have
thread blocks?

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.810
Why can't we just have
a grid of threads,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.450
and each thread just
takes a piece of data

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.330
and does something?

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.010
And this would be normally
fine if all you want to do

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.110
is element-wise operations,
which we'll see.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.770
For example, GeLU is
an activation function

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.460
that applies element-wise.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.760
And threads are pretty natural.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.220
Each thread processes
one element.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:05.260
So it's like Fi for i equals
ranging over your data set.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:13.580
But for operations that involve
communicating with threads,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.740
such as softmax or
matrix multiplication,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.700
this view isn't really enough.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.260
And the reason is that,
well, it could be enough

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:29.300
if you were willing to pay the
cost of writing and reading HBM.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.400
Because then you can still
have-- for example, in a matrix

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.440
we'll see later, you can just
have every element in a cell.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.260
It goes and computes that
element of the matrix.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.940
And it can just
read and write HBM.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:45.040
But as we've noticed,
HBM is very slow,

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.620
so this would not
be a good strategy.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:53.200
So instead, what we should
do is use shared memory,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.560
which is local to SM.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.620
And what the thread block
allows you to think about

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.120
is a collection of
threads that are all going

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.080
to access this shared memory.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.560
So consequently, you can
think about a thread block,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.880
one of these thread
blocks is being scheduled

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.920
on one of these semantic--

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.400
not semantic, streaming
multiprocessors.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.120
And it does its thing.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.560
And what it's
going to do is it's

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.920
going to read a bunch
of data from HBM

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.120
and then process it where
the processing might involve

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.860
communication between the
threads via the shared memory,

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.200
and then writes it back out.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.400
OK.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.920
So this is a critical
piece that comes up.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.180
Later, we'll talk about tiling,
and that's the whole game here.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:49.890
OK, in Triton, in fact, which
we'll see as a primary way

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.110
that we're going to
write kernels these days,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.050
we're going to think
natively in thread blocks.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.810
And I think once you
get the hang of thinking

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:01.090
of thread blocks, this
makes a lot of sense

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.662
and makes your life a
lot easier, as we'll see.

00:07:04.662 --> 00:07:10.530
OK, so the programming model
is fairly simple, I think.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.590
There's threads, thread
blocks, and grids.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.970
I think things get a
bit more complicated,

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.810
is the interaction
between the programming

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.210
model and the hardware.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.670
So the programming model
is actually very nice.

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:28.190
It provides abstraction
of the hardware.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:32.530
When you can write a
kernel, all you have to know

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.510
is that there's a
bunch of thread blocks.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.490
You define them and
then define what

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:42.730
computations each thread within
the block or the thread block

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:43.690
has to do.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:47.690
And those are just writing--
it's like writing Python.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.660
So that part isn't hard.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:51.822
And in fact, if you
don't care about--

00:07:51.822 --> 00:07:53.280
if you just care
about correctness,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.660
that's all you need to know.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.900
But in practice,
the performance is

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.180
very sensitive to the hardware.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.340
And so you need to really
deeply understand the hardware

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:04.722
to obtain high performance.

00:08:04.722 --> 00:08:06.180
And in fact, the
whole reason we're

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.860
talking about GPUs
and kernels is

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.500
that you're trying to
squeeze out performance.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.740
So there's these two
levels here where

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:16.520
you're trying to
understand the computation,

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.520
and that is only a part
of the programming model.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.540
But how fast it runs
is going to be strongly

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.140
dependent on the hardware.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.980
OK, so I'm going to give you
some examples of why this

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:31.900
matters, and some of
this will be review

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.440
from what Tatsu talked about.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.460
But hopefully it will reinforce
some of these concepts.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.659
So I think there will be
around five different examples.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:47.280
And this is just to give you a
flavor of the considerations.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.260
So there's something
called warps,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.560
which I didn't talk about.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:54.360
So in the very
simplified view, warps

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.960
are not really part of this
clean picture of the programming

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:57.460
model.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.680
You have threads and
thread blocks or grids.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.760
Technically, you can
also exceed the warps,

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:08.440
but you don't have to when
you're just programming.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:09.840
So what is a warp?

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.100
So each thread blocks, remember,
is a collection of threads.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.000
But these threads are
actually grouped into warps.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.160
And there's basically
32 threads per warp.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:25.180
So for example, if you have
64 threads in a thread block,

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:29.840
there's two warps, the
first 32 and the second 32.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.980
And as Tatsu
mentioned last time,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.360
all threads within a warp,
all of these T's, must

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.780
execute the same instruction
in lockstep on SM.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.760
So every cycle,
they have to execute

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.560
exactly the same instruction.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.750
And control divergence is when
different threads within a warp

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:51.750
need to actually execute
different instructions.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.750
For example, if you have
branching, if something,

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.730
then A else B.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.370
And then what happens
is that you can only

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:04.150
do the A's in your
threads in your warp,

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.450
and then you do the B.
So this gets sequential.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.810
So this is bad and
inefficient, and that's

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:14.970
why branching is something
you want to generally avoid.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:18.290
One thing that's, I think,
really cool about warps

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:23.028
is that a SM actually is
running multiple warps.

00:10:23.028 --> 00:10:24.570
There's a warp
scheduler, and there's

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:29.270
a bunch of resident warps
that are about to run.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:31.290
Each warp has some--

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.650
threads have registers.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:40.610
And the SM can switch
between them with zero cost.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:43.880
Right, this is not true
in general, like on a CPU.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.660
But this way that it's
designed is to hide latency.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.420
So this is important,
because one

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.440
of the warps is, for
example, reading from HBM,

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:57.080
which remember is
very expensive,

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.880
can take 100 cycles
or something.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.380
You don't want to just sit
around waiting for that warp

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:03.440
to do nothing.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.100
You switch immediately
to another warp

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:09.740
where it can actually do
some tensor core operations.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:13.700
OK, so that's one thing
to note about the warp.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:17.100
And another reason
why warp comes up

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.020
is this idea of occupancy.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:22.620
More specifically,
warp occupancy.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.580
So each thread, just
the hardware constraint

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:31.160
says that each thread can
use at most 255 registers.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:35.220
OK, and so what happens
is that the SM has

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.520
a fixed number of registers.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.200
So the more registers
each thread is using,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.520
the fewer threads you can have.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.070
That's just math.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:50.710
And that can reduce
your occupancy.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.790
But that's not necessarily
bad, because if you

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.790
have fewer threads.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.930
But each thread is doing more
work, that can actually be good.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:01.250
So occupancy is something
that you can measure,

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.750
but it's not necessarily
the larger, the better.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.310
Because there are some
other tradeoffs here.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.350
And in fact, an
example where you

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:15.270
might want to have fewer
threads is this idea

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.310
of thread coarsening,
where let's say

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.630
you have an
element-wise operation

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:27.630
and you can have one thread just
perform work on one element.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.490
OK, so then you have
a lot of threads.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:33.190
But you can also say each thread
processes multiple elements.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.550
Like a constant, maybe eight.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.830
So that gives you
fewer threads, which

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.050
makes scheduling and
these things easier,

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:42.493
but each thread is
doing more work.

00:12:42.493 --> 00:12:43.910
So if your threads
are very light,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:47.330
maybe you want to fatten
them up a little bit.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.290
OK.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:53.590
So just as an example of
what might happen here.

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:56.890
So suppose you have a
block, a thread block

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.450
with 128 threads in it.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.450
And each thread is
using 160 registers.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:04.250
OK.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.270
So there's a
hardware constraint.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:13.450
So the B200s have maximum of
registers of 65,000 per SM.

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:14.970
And it also has
a constraint that

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:20.250
says you can't run more
than 64 warps at a time.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.490
So then you can go and
do some simple math

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:27.450
to compute what is
the occupancy here.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.830
So number of
registers per thread.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.510
This has to be less than 255.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.530
That's fine.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.953
The number of registers
that you're using per block

00:13:35.953 --> 00:13:37.370
is the number of
threads per block

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.200
times the number of
registers per thread.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:40.980
That's about 20,000.

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.820
That means you can run at
most three blocks on your SM

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.380
concurrently.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.220
And then that
corresponds to 12 warps.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:55.140
And because the number
of maximum warps is 64,

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.780
that means you have
an occupancy of 18%.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:04.680
So we're only running 18% the
total number of warps you have.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:08.280
And this is because you have a
lot of registers use per thread.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:14.580
OK, so this is an example of
where I guess this is memory,

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:21.420
is constraining you in terms
of how much compute you can do.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.460
OK, here's another example.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:26.200
Something called bank conflicts.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.500
This applies to shared memory.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.960
So remember, shared
memory is like L1.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:33.820
It's on an SM.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.940
And just the way
the hardware works

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.550
is that shared memory is divided
into 32 banks, each one four

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:41.590
bytes wide.

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:48.030
OK, so these are my 32 banks,
and this is my memory here.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:53.670
Each bank has many
elements here.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:58.710
So there's a constraint
that says every clock cycle,

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:02.990
each bank can only be accessed
by one thread, at most

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:04.790
one thread.

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:06.450
Assuming that's not a location.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:08.990
So for example, if
you can't access

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:14.390
this location and this
location at the same time.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:17.470
Which means that if you
have multiple threads that

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:20.030
are trying to access
the same bank,

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:23.430
the accesses have
to be serialized.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.770
And this is called
a bank conflict.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.270
In the worst case
example, imagine

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.910
this were a matrix that
was laid out like this.

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:34.810
And you had some operation where
32 threads, you think, wow,

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.340
this 32 is so great.

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:38.040
I can massively
parallelize this,

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.540
and you try to all
access this first column.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.880
They're just going
to just wait in line.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.600
And this is a 32-way
bank conflict,

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:50.200
which is the worst possible
setting you can be in.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:52.460
Now you could say, well,
OK, why did you do that?

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:54.340
Just like access
rows, of course.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.740
But this is unavoidable,
because, for example,

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.880
if you're doing a
matmul, you have

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:02.500
to access rows of one matrix
and columns of another matrix.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.200
And sometimes you do
transposes, so you can't always

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:12.480
just get away with choosing
the order in which you go down.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.260
For element-wise
operations, it's fine.

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:15.900
You can go in any order.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:17.920
But if you're doing
matmuls, you can't

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:22.760
control for every matrix
which row major or column

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:24.350
major you're doing.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.600
There are some solutions
here, which I won't get into.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:29.800
Something called swizzling,
which arranges your shared

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:33.440
memory so that when
you're going through,

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:37.120
you can avoid bank conflicts.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:40.260
OK, so that's another
consideration.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:43.760
And when you're profiling, you
can look at the bank conflicts,

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.240
you can look at occupancy.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:49.980
And you can see
what's happening.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:51.980
A final note which--

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:53.340
actually, two more things.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.440
So memory coalescing,
which you talked about.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.340
So I'll just really
quickly remind

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.540
people what this is about.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:05.619
So when you have 32 threads in a
warp and they try to access HBM,

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:07.819
the memory accesses
actually get combined

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:14.200
into a transaction of 128 bytes,
which are called cache lines.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.619
And it goes and
fetches it all at once.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:21.420
So imagine you have
memory laid out like this.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.920
This is 32.

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:34.790
And in the best case, which
is called full coalescing,

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:37.810
all the threads are accessing
the same cache line.

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:42.770
So you have thread 1 accessing
M00, thread 2 accessing M01,

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:43.710
and so on.

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:46.390
Which means that
all at once, you're

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:49.230
going to grab this
entire cache line.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.390
Right, and whereas if
you go down the columns,

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:59.070
then you're going to
fetch a lot of memory

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.310
here, which you're
not going to use.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:04.670
Same for the second
row and so on.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:08.468
And so this feels similar
to bank conflicts,

00:18:08.468 --> 00:18:10.010
but it's a very
different constraint.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.670
And this is about shared
memory, and this is about HBM.

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:15.470
OK.

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:19.270
OK, so final thing,
block occupancy.

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:23.550
So thread blocks are,
remember, scheduled onto SMS.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.670
But we live in a finite--

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:29.570
logically, you can define as
many thread blocks as you want.

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:33.100
But physically on chip, you only
have a certain number of SMs.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.760
For example, 148.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.000
So if you launch
160 thread blocks,

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.800
then you can only
schedule 148 of them.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.760
And then you have to
wait until they're done,

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:45.520
and then you schedule the 12.

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:47.800
But what happens
if you schedule 12

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:52.800
is that there's a bunch of SMs
are just not doing anything.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.640
So that's called low occupancy.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.560
When the last wave
of thread blocks

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:05.500
gets fewer than the total
maximum number of thread blocks.

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.640
OK, so in general,
maybe it's a good idea

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:13.800
to make the number of thread
blocks divide the SMs.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:18.440
So maybe just to
summarize here, there

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.600
is a very elegant
programming model

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:23.120
where you have a
grid of thread blocks

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:28.200
and the thread blocks have
individual threads in them.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.960
And in terms of the memory,
HBM is global to everyone.

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:35.460
Shared memory is local
to a thread block.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.460
And registers are
local to a thread.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.040
But again, all the details
of the hardware-- warps,

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:44.540
bank conflicts, memory
coalescing, occupancy--

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:49.540
really determine performance.

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:56.600
And many of these details are
hard to know, because, I mean,

00:19:56.600 --> 00:20:00.320
the profiling tells you
a bunch of information.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:04.020
But you have to know
exactly how many SMs there

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:08.520
are and exactly the
sizing of everything.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.540
Sometimes the scheduler
does something

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:14.060
you don't really have
control over what it's doing.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:19.980
So it's a little messier
than the programming model.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.920
OK, I will stop
there for questions.

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:23.483
Yeah?

00:20:23.483 --> 00:20:24.400
Yes, a quick question.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:27.420
So Cam, is there a
scenario in which you

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:31.590
one can share with them
or-- like, for example,

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:35.450
in the problem that we see 140
SM [INAUDIBLE] blocks, right.

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:39.470
And we have to launch
into two different ways.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:44.150
Is there no way in which
a block can share an SM?

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:48.830
Yeah, so the question is,
can a block share an SM?

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:55.510
I think the issue is if you're
doing things right, then the--

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:57.630
I guess it depends on the block.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.330
If you have a block
that's for example,

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:03.310
using most of the
tensor cores on the SM,

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:06.550
then putting another
block there isn't really

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.950
going to speed things up.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:13.550
I think fundamentally,
there is this jagged problem

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:16.470
here of unevenness.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.830
Because the blocks
have to stay together.

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:21.690
So you can't take this and
spread it out over here.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:28.700
The thing you would do is
to change your blocksize.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.440
So you change the
number of blocks

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:33.180
so you don't get this tail here.

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.140
Any other questions?

00:21:44.584 --> 00:21:49.480
OK, so let's move on.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:55.680
Hopefully you guys are getting
more comfortable with GPUs now.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.880
So now I'm going to talk about
benchmarking and profiling.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:02.400
I'm not going to
actually maybe say

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.920
that much in terms of content.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:09.200
But I do want to emphasize
the philosophy here, which is,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.400
here's a recipe for success.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.460
You benchmark and
profile your code.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:16.980
You make changes, and you
benchmark your profile,

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.200
your code again.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.020
And the reason I'm doing
benchmarking and profiling

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:24.490
as opposed to teaching
you try and earlier

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:27.370
is because you should
always just measure

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:31.170
what's going on in your
code and figure out

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:36.370
what the bottlenecks are before
you start writing kernels.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:43.250
OK, so benchmarking is
basically how long things take.

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:46.370
And it gives you just
this end to end time.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:49.490
It doesn't tell you
where things are spent.

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:52.770
But nonetheless, it's pretty
useful, because ultimately,

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:56.850
that's the thing that you
care about, how long things

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:58.650
are running for.

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:01.005
And it also gives you,
because it distills things

00:23:01.005 --> 00:23:03.630
into one number, you can see how
things are scaling, let's say,

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.610
with dimension.

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:09.370
So there's a nice
tool for benchmarking.

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:12.390
But because this class is
language models from scratch,

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:14.890
I'm going to do it from scratch.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:19.850
But I think I'm doing it just
to highlight a few gotchas

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:21.470
with benchmarking.

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:26.470
So let's say we have
this operation, matrix

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:28.630
multiplication.

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:32.630
So run operation 2 is this
wrapper that basically

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:36.070
instantiates two random square
matrices of size dimension

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:37.270
by dimension.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:39.990
And then it returns a function
to perform the operation.

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:43.550
OK, so matmul, if you
call this, basically

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:47.350
does the matmul of those
two random matrices.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:49.970
OK, the random
matrices are generated,

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:53.150
it's just like
multiplying the two.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:55.990
OK, so how do you benchmark?

00:23:55.990 --> 00:24:00.150
So the naive thing to do
is just start time, run it,

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:01.910
and then stop time.

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:04.050
But there's a few things.

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:07.090
One is that always remember
to do your warm-up.

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:11.450
I think I mentioned this
on the second lecture,

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:15.350
but I think it's
worth emphasizing.

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:19.350
And this is because if you have
some things are lazily compiled

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:24.880
and you want to just make
sure that that thing is--

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.023
that time doesn't factor into.

00:24:27.023 --> 00:24:28.440
Because most of
the time, you care

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.520
about how fast something
is, because you're

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:32.020
going to run it
over and over again,

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:35.360
so the initial conditions
don't really matter.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:38.400
And then you often want
to time it multiple times

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.440
because there is some variance.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:47.640
The proper way to time things is
to use these CUDA events that--

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:52.520
a start event and an end event,
which you can call record on.

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:57.760
Actually do the computation and
then hit the end event record.

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:01.200
And then remember to synchronize
to wait for the CUDA threads

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.080
to finish, because
everything on the GPU

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:05.780
is happening if
you have an async.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:09.040
And this is a
synchronization barrier.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.360
And then you record the time.

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:17.800
OK, and then you can do
this again, and then again.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:21.590
And then here, we're
just taking the average.

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:26.290
If you are being
very particular,

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:30.450
you probably want to maybe
look at the whole distribution,

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:32.430
the P95 or whatever.

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:35.690
But we'll just do
the average here.

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:38.450
And then one thing that you can
do with benchmarking is you can,

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:42.170
let's say, scale
up your matrices

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:45.410
and see how the time changes.

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:51.730
And you can see that in this
case, matrix multiplication, as

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:55.090
expected, it should
grow cubically.

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:58.770
But notice that there is this
flaw where up until you get up

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:03.350
to almost 2,000
dimensional matrices,

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:06.210
things are basically a constant.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:10.010
Right, and this is because,
as we've discussed,

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:15.450
the shapes of these GPUs are
built for fairly large matrix

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:16.430
multiplications.

00:26:16.430 --> 00:26:19.910
And if you have a
2 by 2 matrix, it's

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:23.190
going to be very inefficient.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:25.180
OK.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:29.110
So really quickly on profiling.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:31.810
Profiling tells you where
time is actually spent.

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:34.110
Hopefully all of you are
familiar with profiling

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.190
and are doing it.

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:38.870
Maybe less obviously,
profiling, even

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:40.550
if you don't care
about the time,

00:26:40.550 --> 00:26:43.470
helps you figure
out what's actually

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:44.690
happening under the hood.

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:46.350
Because especially with
these high level languages,

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:49.210
you write some code, and then
it runs and you get some result.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.870
And sometimes it can be
just good to understand

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:53.670
what's actually going on.

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:55.570
So PyTorch has a
built-in profiler.

00:26:55.570 --> 00:26:57.390
In your assignment,
you'll use Nsight,

00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:01.230
which gives you more details.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:05.950
But we're going to skip that
in the interest of time.

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:13.510
So let's start with just if you
add two numbers and-- or sorry,

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:17.800
two tensors in
PyTorch, again, a run

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:21.440
operation creates
two random matrices

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.720
and applies the operation.

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:27.120
So in profiling, I'm warming up.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:32.840
And then I'm just putting this
context of-- the profiling

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.320
context and doing the run.

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:41.880
And then let's look at what the
profile looks like for just a

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:45.600
plus b in PyTorch.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:48.740
Normally doing PyTorch, you
probably don't think about it.

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:51.780
It's like, OK, well, these
two tensors just get added.

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:54.480
So what's actually going
on underneath the hood?

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:57.800
Right, so if you look at the
things that it's calling,

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:05.640
there is this long name,
kernel at CUDA functor add.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:11.740
So this is basically a
kernel that adds to tensors.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:14.360
And the times aren't going
to be that interesting,

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:15.470
because I'm only adding.

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:18.010
So that's going to
take 100% of the time.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:20.290
But this tells you that,
well, underneath the hood

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:24.010
there is this thing called add.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:25.270
What about matmul?

00:28:28.010 --> 00:28:32.210
In PyTorch, I'm doing a at b.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.890
Similarly, there
is this long name

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:39.570
that describes this
particular matmul kernel.

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:46.730
Cutlass f32, f32,
64x64x16, and so on.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:49.950
Notice that if you
change the dimensions--

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:53.970
so now I'm doing a
128 by 128 matmul--

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:56.450
you get actually
a different one.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:29:02.650
If you look closely, that this
is 64x64x16, this is 32x32x16.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:07.010
OK, so underneath
the hood, PyTorch,

00:29:07.010 --> 00:29:08.750
it looks like you're
doing just at.

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.792
But underneath the hood,
there could be all sorts

00:29:10.792 --> 00:29:13.780
of things that are happening.

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:15.658
So observations.

00:29:15.658 --> 00:29:17.700
Here, you can see which
CUDA kernels are actually

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:18.520
being called.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:21.640
These are generally the ones
that have the long names.

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:23.660
And different CUDA kernels
are invoked depending

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:27.500
on the tensor dimensions.

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:29.140
The name actually
tells you something

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:31.500
about the
implementation as well.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.620
So this name.

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:38.460
So cutlass is the NVIDIA CUDA
library for linear algebra.

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:42.320
SM100 corresponds to the
Blackwell architecture.

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:44.020
So this is a kernel
that's specifically

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:46.500
designed for Blackwell.

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:48.700
This is f32.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:54.800
And then 64x64x16 is
the shape of the tile,

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:57.540
which we're going to talk about
later when we talk about tiling

00:29:57.540 --> 00:30:01.740
in the context of matmuls.

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:05.760
OK, so that's
benchmarking profiling.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:08.087
Just remember to do it.

00:30:08.087 --> 00:30:09.920
I think we make you do
it on the assignment,

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:12.480
so you have no choice.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.360
OK.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:21.320
So let's apply this to another
example here on the GeLU.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:27.080
So remember, the GeLU
activation function

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:32.200
is this function, which is a
typical non-linearity that's

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:32.800
used.

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:39.240
Often, it's approximated with
this tanh approximation, which

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.920
is more computer friendly.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:49.320
OK, so naively, if you
implement the GeLU,

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:53.240
you can do it in
PyTorch just like this.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:56.680
So you take a tensor, you
just take this equation

00:30:56.680 --> 00:31:00.040
and you just put it
into PyTorch, OK.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:01.440
That's fine.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:03.560
And you get some number.

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.440
PyTorch also has a built in.

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:12.130
If you call functional GeLU, you
can get that version as well.

00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:16.250
And you can check
that they're the same.

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:21.130
You can run the
two GeLU versions

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:26.170
and make sure that the answer
is the same for a random input.

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:31.190
There's also something that
people probably have discovered.

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:33.750
If you haven't
encountered this, this

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:35.770
is an important thing to know.

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:37.850
Is that you can take
any PyTorch function,

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:40.330
you call torch.compile
on it, and it

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:44.330
generates another function.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:47.810
And it does the same thing.

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:52.610
OK, so we have three
horses in this race.

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:56.050
We have the naive
implementation.

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:57.550
We have the built-in
implementation.

00:31:57.550 --> 00:31:59.050
We have the compiled
implementation.

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:00.650
So we can benchmark them.

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:02.870
Naive takes three.

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:09.220
I guess this is 3.75.

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:12.700
Built-in is much
faster and compiled

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:16.700
is much faster as well, but
not maybe as quite as fast

00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:18.460
as built-in.

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:19.380
OK.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.940
So what's happening here?

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:26.740
Why are these things different?

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:29.100
They all compute
the same answer,

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:33.820
but they have wildly different
performance characteristics.

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:36.260
And so here we can
pull up the profiler

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:39.300
and see what's actually
going on underneath the hood.

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:45.380
So if you look at the
naive GeLU here and just do

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:47.385
a profile, this--

00:32:47.385 --> 00:32:48.760
something's wrong
with this view,

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.560
so I'm going to go to this.

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:59.300
So you see that the profile
shows how time is being spent.

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:03.800
There's a bunch of different
kernels on this binary functor

00:33:03.800 --> 00:33:06.580
unary add.

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:09.680
tanh is a kernel here.

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:13.040
And this corresponds to the
fact that in PyTorch, when

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:15.080
we write the PyTorch
expression, you

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:16.880
look at the
computation graph, and

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:18.840
each primitive in
the computation

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:21.600
graph is actually
realized in the kernel.

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:23.410
Right.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:29.080
And the reason this is slow is
that when you launch a kernel,

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:32.880
the kernel has to read from HBM.

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:36.160
Pull it all the way over to
your SM, do the computation,

00:33:36.160 --> 00:33:37.560
and write it back.

00:33:37.560 --> 00:33:41.040
And then the kernel
picks that up from HBM

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.180
and then writes it back,
and so on and so forth.

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:46.980
So you're doing a lot of reads
and writes back and forth.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:49.200
Because between
kernel invocations,

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:54.160
things have to go back to HBM.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:58.880
So if you look at what
the built-in is doing,

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:00.560
this is actually not
that interesting.

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:02.980
There's this GeLU CUDA
kernel implementation,

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:05.500
which is a single kernel that
just implements the GeLU.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:08.429
And so why does this exist?

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:11.778
Well, because people use GeLU.

00:34:11.778 --> 00:34:13.570
So someone wrote a
kernel for it and put it

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:15.610
in the standard library.

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:21.210
I mean, there's nothing magical.

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:24.370
So compilation is
really interesting here.

00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:26.949
I'm not going to say too
much about how it works,

00:34:26.949 --> 00:34:33.050
but it's really, I think,
a fascinating topic where

00:34:33.050 --> 00:34:38.710
you can take a naive
implementation, which, remember,

00:34:38.710 --> 00:34:41.130
it's in PyTorch, it has
a computation graph.

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:42.850
And run a compiler.

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:46.530
Which basically, if you look
at what's underneath the hood,

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:48.250
it's just a single kernel.

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:50.730
And this is because
it's figured out

00:34:50.730 --> 00:34:56.610
to look at the computation
graph and essentially write

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:59.142
that kernel in Triton.

00:34:59.142 --> 00:35:01.350
So you can see that this is
actually a Triton kernel.

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:06.100
OK, so naive implementation.

00:35:06.100 --> 00:35:08.460
Multiple kernels requires
multiple reads and writes

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:10.020
to and from HBM.

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:12.940
There's no kernel fusion here.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:14.820
This is slow.

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:18.100
The built-in compiler
version, there's one kernel.

00:35:18.100 --> 00:35:19.980
Basically all the
operation in the GeLU

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:22.260
have been fused together
into one kernel.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:27.980
So you read from HBM once, you
write to HBM once per element.

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:31.880
And you see that the compiled
kernel is a Triton kernel.

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:37.700
OK, so that maybe
is a good segue

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:41.240
to talk about what this
Triton thing is all about.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:41.820
Yeah?

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.380
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:51.620 --> 00:35:52.180
Let's see.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:54.880
I mean, I guess it says
CUDA kernel implementation,

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:57.120
so I imagine someone
wrote it in CUDA.

00:36:03.236 --> 00:36:07.510
OK, any other questions?

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:09.190
Yeah?

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:15.710
Why is Triton kernel
faster than this one?

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:18.650
So why is the Triton
kernel faster?

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:23.870
So a Triton kernel is actually
not faster in this case.

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:25.230
Oh, sorry.

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:30.930
The compiled kernel
is one Triton kernel,

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:32.910
and this is slower
than the built-in.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:34.470
Yeah.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:38.990
I think last year when I did
this, it was actually closer.

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:42.990
But these things change, and
it's very hardware dependent.

00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:48.090
And I think none of this
is terribly optimized.

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:51.750
This is just giving you
the general idea here.

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:57.190
OK, so let's write
some Triton kernels.

00:36:57.190 --> 00:37:01.130
So remember our
programming model.

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:04.030
You have a bunch of threads
organized by Thread groups,

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:07.970
a thread blocks, and there's
a grid of thread blocks.

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:13.250
And if you were to write in
CUDA, which was originally

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:16.290
developed by NVIDIA, and
that's been for years the thing

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:19.010
that you do when
you write kernels,

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:22.190
the mental model is,
what does each thread do?

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:25.450
So you write a piece of
code which essentially

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.530
has some ID that
identifies which

00:37:27.530 --> 00:37:31.410
thread you're talking about,
and it just executes the code.

00:37:31.410 --> 00:37:35.330
So the nice thing about this is
that it's very closely related

00:37:35.330 --> 00:37:40.810
to what is actually happening
underneath the hood.

00:37:40.810 --> 00:37:47.050
And it's fine-grained, gives
you fine-grained control.

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:50.850
But there's cons here,
which is that, remember,

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:56.090
all these threads are
in a thread block.

00:37:56.090 --> 00:38:00.580
And some operations require
them to communicate.

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:05.300
So what has to happen is that
they have to synchronize.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:09.480
And they read from
HBM all at once.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.460
And they have to synchronize
and do the computation.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.780
And you have to basically
do that bookkeeping.

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:20.420
So if you were doing all
element-wise operations,

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:21.800
this CUDA is just fine.

00:38:21.800 --> 00:38:24.720
It doesn't really matter.

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.180
But as you get more
complex operations,

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:32.140
then try and provide some
value of the abstraction.

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:35.000
So Triton was
developed by OpenAI.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.780
I think by now, it's
been pretty standard.

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:42.500
You basically specify what
each thread block does.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:45.593
Generally it's powerful enough,
especially for this class

00:38:45.593 --> 00:38:46.760
that you're getting started.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:51.260
If you really go and want to
exploit every single new feature

00:38:51.260 --> 00:38:53.540
of the latest
hardware, it might not

00:38:53.540 --> 00:38:55.120
give you the full flexibility.

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:59.550
But let's not worry about that.

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:02.150
And the conceptual framework
to think about in Triton

00:39:02.150 --> 00:39:05.830
is that you're thinking
about, what does a block do?

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:08.650
A block is going to load
data into shared memory,

00:39:08.650 --> 00:39:13.030
operate it on, and write
it back to global memory.

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:16.030
So in some sense, these
blocks are intermediate point

00:39:16.030 --> 00:39:19.870
between thinking about what
individual elements are doing

00:39:19.870 --> 00:39:22.570
as well as thinking about
the general operation.

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:25.310
And so in PyTorch, you basically
define these huge matrices

00:39:25.310 --> 00:39:27.010
and you say multiply
them together.

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:29.045
And that's the atomic operation.

00:39:29.045 --> 00:39:30.670
And a lot of what
you're thinking about

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:34.070
is, how do I get things
into big matmuls?

00:39:34.070 --> 00:39:37.510
Right, and at some
level, Triton is

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:43.110
a hybrid between that and
the individual elements,

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:44.350
as we'll see.

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:50.590
OK, so let's start with
the GeLU example here.

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:55.030
So let's define an 8,000
dimensional vector and start

00:39:55.030 --> 00:39:57.050
writing some Triton.

00:39:57.050 --> 00:40:03.310
OK, so Triton is basically
going to be you write Python.

00:40:03.310 --> 00:40:06.530
How many of you have
written Triton before?

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:07.670
Just a show of hands.

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:10.130
OK, OK.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:16.090
Not that many, which is good,
because then you won't be bored.

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:22.090
OK, so this is normal PyTorch.

00:40:22.090 --> 00:40:23.890
Right, there's no Triton here.

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:26.970
But I'm just preparing.

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:31.330
And so what I'm going to
do is I take this tensor,

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:35.890
and I'm going to allocate
an output tensor.

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:39.990
OK, because in Triton, we're not
thinking functionally anymore.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:41.790
We're just thinking
about moving.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:44.810
You have to explicitly
read and write.

00:40:44.810 --> 00:40:47.030
So there's no returning value.

00:40:47.030 --> 00:40:48.610
So I'm going to
allocate the output

00:40:48.610 --> 00:40:52.210
tensor, which I'm going to--
the kernel's going to write to.

00:40:52.210 --> 00:40:58.780
And then so this tensor can
be arbitrarily big, right.

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:03.140
And I can't generally
fit this all into one SM,

00:41:03.140 --> 00:41:07.300
because it's just too big.

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:10.580
So I need to break
it up into blocks.

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:16.380
So what I'm going to do is
you can think about this x

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:18.300
as being this array.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:20.080
I'm going to chop
it up into blocks.

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:22.900
So the total number
of elements is 8,000.

00:41:22.900 --> 00:41:27.380
I'm just going to set the
blocksize to 1,024 for now.

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:31.380
And then I have eight blocks.

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:34.120
OK.

00:41:34.120 --> 00:41:42.980
So then I'm going to use this
weird syntax to call the kernel.

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:45.900
So Triton GeLU kernel.

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:53.190
This basically in brackets
tells me essentially the shape

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:54.630
of the grid.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:59.490
So basically, this says
the grid has num blocks.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:01.590
Blocks.

00:42:01.590 --> 00:42:05.270
And I'm going to, for
every one of these blocks,

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:08.790
invoke this function,
Triton GeLU kernel, which

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:10.370
I'll talk about in a bit.

00:42:10.370 --> 00:42:17.110
I'm going to pass in an x, y
num elements and the blocksize.

00:42:17.110 --> 00:42:18.900
OK.

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:21.465
All right, so
unfortunately, I'm not

00:42:21.465 --> 00:42:23.090
going to be able to
trace through this,

00:42:23.090 --> 00:42:28.050
so I'll just show you what
the code looks like here.

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:30.770
So let me get rid of that.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:37.170
OK, so this is probably the
simplest kernel you can imagine.

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:41.710
So when you are looking
at the triton gelu kernel,

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:46.950
now this is before
we had x and y.

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:49.150
So now these are pointers.

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:52.660
Right, you can think about
these as just integers,

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:54.440
they're addresses.

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:57.040
So you have to get
comfortable with that.

00:42:57.040 --> 00:43:00.960
And then we have the number
of elements and the blocksize,

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:09.680
which are basically actually
passed in from here.

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:14.480
OK, so the way to think about
it is that for every block,

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:20.240
we're going to have this
function being called.

00:43:20.240 --> 00:43:25.680
And what happens is the
first thing I'm going to do

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:27.520
is I'm going to wake up.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:30.880
This block wakes up
and say, who am I?

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:33.760
Well, the program
ID is-- basically

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:35.860
the PID is basically
identifying the block.

00:43:35.860 --> 00:43:40.600
So PID would be 0 for this
block 1, 2, and 3 here.

00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:43.360
OK, so now I have to
figure out what data

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:45.360
I'm going to operate on.

00:43:45.360 --> 00:43:48.280
So that's PID times blocksize.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:52.580
So this is the offset
into x pointer.

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:56.200
So if PID is 0, then start is 0.

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:59.360
If PID is 1, then
start is blocksize.

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:03.520
If PID is 2, then it's 2
times blocksize, and so on.

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:04.020
OK.

00:44:04.020 --> 00:44:09.320
So now, I'm going to figure
out the span I'm operating on.

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:11.980
So offsets is start plus.

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:17.260
This is the Triton library.

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:21.100
A range 0 blocksize, which
conceptually gives you

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:24.780
the integers 0 through
blocksize minus 1.

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:29.300
So offsets is going
to be essentially

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:31.220
for-- let's say
block 1, it's going

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:33.900
to be blocksize blocksize
plus 1 all the way to blocks

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:36.760
2 times blocksize minus 1.

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:45.440
OK, so in this case, num
elements divides by blocksize.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.080
But in general,
that's not the case.

00:44:47.080 --> 00:44:49.790
So you'll often see in train
code, there's this masking.

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:54.030
Which says, well, sometimes if
the-- let's say the tensor only

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:55.350
goes up to here.

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:58.350
Then I'm going to
form a mask, which

00:44:58.350 --> 00:45:01.550
is going to be true
up until that point

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:04.150
and false after that point.

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:07.170
And for blocks that are
not the final block,

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:11.270
it's going to be just all ones.

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:13.690
OK, so now I've done the setup.

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:15.830
What I do is I read.

00:45:15.830 --> 00:45:18.230
And this is basically
pointer arithmetic.

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:20.150
So x pointer, remember,
is the integer

00:45:20.150 --> 00:45:23.190
that specifies the memory
location of where x is.

00:45:23.190 --> 00:45:25.230
I'm going to add
offsets to that,

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:30.390
which gives me the first
blocksize number of elements

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:32.190
there.

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:34.350
According to the mask,
if I'm masked out,

00:45:34.350 --> 00:45:38.110
then I don't read it.

00:45:38.110 --> 00:45:44.670
And then now I think you can
just think about this a vector.

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:49.040
And then you do your
normal computation.

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:52.720
And then you get y.

00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:55.280
And y is the same size as x.

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:58.840
And then you do
tl store y pointer

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:02.920
plus offsets y and the mask.

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:07.920
OK, so this load loads
from HBM, does some stuff,

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:09.740
and then it writes back to HBM.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:17.120
OK, I'm going to stop there
and take any questions

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:22.000
about this first Triton kernel.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:22.800
Yeah?

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:26.493
[INAUDIBLE]

00:46:33.220 --> 00:46:33.720
Yeah.

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:36.360
So the difference is, what is
the difference between CUDA?

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:41.200
And for this element wise, it
looks pretty much the same,

00:46:41.200 --> 00:46:42.000
right.

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:45.450
And in fact, CUDA is
even, I think, simpler,

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:47.270
because it really
is element wise.

00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:50.870
You wake up, and you
identify the thread.

00:46:50.870 --> 00:46:53.930
And then you just
operate on that element.

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.810
Now the only thing
here is that it's

00:46:55.810 --> 00:47:00.330
like the vectorized version,
where you have a block

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:03.290
and you operate on that block.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:06.330
Later, we'll see that
operating on blocks

00:47:06.330 --> 00:47:09.650
is doing more than-- if
you do something more

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:11.570
than element-wise,
then CUDA is going

00:47:11.570 --> 00:47:14.530
to be a lot more
annoying to work with.

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:15.710
So a bunch of questions.

00:47:15.710 --> 00:47:17.250
Yeah, back there.

00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:21.370
How does this relate to if you
wanted to use the tensor units?

00:47:21.370 --> 00:47:25.730
So how is this related to if you
want to use the tensor units?

00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:28.890
So I'll later show you
what this code actually

00:47:28.890 --> 00:47:31.090
compiles down into.

00:47:31.090 --> 00:47:35.050
And maybe I'll get back to that.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:37.790
But the short answer is
that you don't control that.

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:43.190
The hardware figures
out where to put things.

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:44.070
Yeah?

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:45.670
Can you actually
walk through what's

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:51.070
happening at tf.load from
HBM to share to register?

00:47:51.070 --> 00:47:54.990
What's the step by step where
data is actually flowing?

00:47:54.990 --> 00:47:57.670
Yeah, so the question is, what
is actually happening when

00:47:57.670 --> 00:48:02.990
this statement is executed?

00:48:02.990 --> 00:48:07.270
So the short version is that
this is in some sense a lie.

00:48:07.270 --> 00:48:10.390
Right, it's not like
the GPU actually

00:48:10.390 --> 00:48:13.490
calls the Triton
library on the GPU

00:48:13.490 --> 00:48:15.430
and it's actually
executing this code.

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:18.510
This is basically
for our consumption

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:21.310
to specify the computation.

00:48:21.310 --> 00:48:23.550
The compiler takes this,
as we'll see later,

00:48:23.550 --> 00:48:25.270
and writes it into
something called ptx,

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:29.130
and then it will
actually do the work.

00:48:32.990 --> 00:48:35.270
So that's mechanically
what's happening,

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:38.030
which I'll get to later.

00:48:38.030 --> 00:48:42.360
If you're asking about
the conceptual question,

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:49.000
the way to think about
this is that this--

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:55.080
x pointer is a memory
location in HBM.

00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:58.900
And this basically specifies
a range of memory locations.

00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:00.480
And load takes those
memory locations

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:04.560
and returns the data
associated with them.

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:08.160
Here, I have a local
variable called x.

00:49:08.160 --> 00:49:10.960
In practice, this is
going to be generally

00:49:10.960 --> 00:49:16.360
a register or shared memory.

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:20.840
Triton figures out
what to do there.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:22.800
[INAUDIBLE]

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:26.040
--at the threat level.

00:49:26.040 --> 00:49:28.220
When this is invoked,
it's not really clear.

00:49:28.220 --> 00:49:33.620
Did I line up what is
going to be in shared

00:49:33.620 --> 00:49:35.820
and going to be at the
register level beforehand,

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:39.250
or is it happening
now, which is way late?

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:41.250
And so then so that's
just where all the threads

00:49:41.250 --> 00:49:45.870
are sitting idle to get
a memory to flush in.

00:49:48.930 --> 00:49:52.252
I'm sure it's ready to
execute so that we're not

00:49:52.252 --> 00:49:53.710
sitting there
twiddling our thumbs,

00:49:53.710 --> 00:49:55.730
just waiting for data
to come in from HBM.

00:49:55.730 --> 00:49:56.470
Yeah, yeah.

00:49:56.470 --> 00:49:59.110
So the question is, when does
this actually get executed,

00:49:59.110 --> 00:50:01.370
and doesn't this block?

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:03.370
Let me try to come
back to this question

00:50:03.370 --> 00:50:05.890
when I show you the ptx,
and maybe hopefully it

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:09.770
will provide a bit more
context on what's happening.

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:11.270
OK, any other questions?

00:50:15.490 --> 00:50:19.150
OK, so all the kernels are going
to look something like this.

00:50:19.150 --> 00:50:23.010
So I do want to make
sure people understand

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:26.370
just the general form, which
is that you have generally

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:28.250
your inputs, your outputs.

00:50:28.250 --> 00:50:30.560
You wake up, you
figure out which

00:50:30.560 --> 00:50:31.810
index you're going to look at.

00:50:31.810 --> 00:50:32.670
You read.

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:37.050
You do some stuff, and
then you write to HBM.

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:41.050
OK.

00:50:41.050 --> 00:50:48.350
So let's talk about ptx briefly.

00:50:48.350 --> 00:50:50.550
So let's see, does this work?

00:50:50.550 --> 00:50:53.070
Does this link work?

00:50:53.070 --> 00:51:00.790
OK, so when you write
Triton, the compiler

00:51:00.790 --> 00:51:04.350
generates ptx code, which is
this intermediate assembly

00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:08.190
language for GPUs.

00:51:08.190 --> 00:51:11.290
I am obviously not going
to go through all of this,

00:51:11.290 --> 00:51:17.630
but just to give you the
flavor of what this looks like.

00:51:17.630 --> 00:51:20.970
Let me actually start
with the observation.

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:22.550
So a few things.

00:51:22.550 --> 00:51:30.150
So this is now what a
thread is actually doing.

00:51:30.150 --> 00:51:35.710
Not a thread block, because
that's been compiled away.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:41.440
And so if you look at--

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:43.360
a few notes here.

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:50.200
So this LD global is
basically saying load from HBM

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:51.740
into some registers.

00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:59.080
And the registers are denoted as
the r are integer registers, fr,

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:01.760
floating point registers.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:05.860
And then you have statements
like mov 0 into r5,

00:52:05.860 --> 00:52:09.040
mov 0 into r6, and so on.

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:10.660
And then you have
multiplication.

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:14.360
You're going to multiply this
register by this constant

00:52:14.360 --> 00:52:17.840
and then put it into this.

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:19.900
So this code gets executed.

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:25.060
And at the bottom, you
should see global store.

00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:28.600
So this is writing
back into the HBM.

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:30.380
So this gets executed.

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:36.810
This is actually the code that
gets executed on a thread.

00:52:36.810 --> 00:52:42.690
And Triton is basically
a layer up above that.

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:47.450
One other thing to notice is
that you see all these blocks

00:52:47.450 --> 00:52:49.410
and what's going on there.

00:52:49.410 --> 00:52:51.850
And what's going on
there is I alluded

00:52:51.850 --> 00:52:55.170
to thread coarsening, which
is that this is one thread.

00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:57.550
But rather than processing
a single element,

00:52:57.550 --> 00:52:59.630
it's actually processing
eight elements.

00:52:59.630 --> 00:53:02.153
So the compiler decided that,
well, actually, this thread

00:53:02.153 --> 00:53:03.070
is pretty lightweight.

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:04.730
It doesn't do that
much, so let's just

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:07.930
try to thicken it
up a little bit.

00:53:07.930 --> 00:53:14.970
So looking at ptx can give you
some of appreciation for what's

00:53:14.970 --> 00:53:18.010
going on underneath the hood.

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:18.570
Yeah?

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:21.210
So it makes this for
each individual--

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:23.690
[INAUDIBLE]

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:30.670
So the question is, does it
make this for each thread?

00:53:30.670 --> 00:53:33.450
So this is compiled once.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:39.740
And it's the same piece of
code that each thread runs.

00:53:39.740 --> 00:53:42.380
And the way that the
thread distinguishes

00:53:42.380 --> 00:53:45.580
itself is that this
piece of code gets

00:53:45.580 --> 00:53:52.180
passed basically the thread ID.

00:53:52.180 --> 00:53:56.140
So here, the cta.x
is the block index,

00:53:56.140 --> 00:53:58.980
and tid.x is the thread index.

00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:03.300
So this basically, if this
piece of code is running,

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:07.020
this tells you
which block I'm in.

00:54:07.020 --> 00:54:12.520
And tid.x tells you which
thread inside that block I'm in.

00:54:16.840 --> 00:54:17.340
OK.

00:54:21.100 --> 00:54:22.500
OK.

00:54:22.500 --> 00:54:23.560
Any other questions?

00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:26.740
And I'm trying to
figure out how to--

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:27.240
OK.

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:39.840
OK, so that's generally a flavor
of your first Triton kernel

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:42.920
load from HBM compute
right back to HBM.

00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:50.880
And then we saw the ptx, which
is the grungy what actually

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:52.840
happens underneath the hood.

00:54:52.840 --> 00:54:57.480
There's still a lot of things
that are not specified in ptx.

00:54:57.480 --> 00:55:00.800
Like, for example, which
SM things are operating on

00:55:00.800 --> 00:55:03.240
and the warps and everything.

00:55:03.240 --> 00:55:05.180
A lot of those are
hardware controlled,

00:55:05.180 --> 00:55:07.340
so you don't even see.

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:09.040
Yeah?

00:55:09.040 --> 00:55:13.480
So ptx code is generated by
the compiler, not something

00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:16.760
that you would normally code?

00:55:16.760 --> 00:55:18.960
It looks like it's
assembly code.

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:24.180
Yeah, so the question is, is ptx
just generated by the compiler?

00:55:24.180 --> 00:55:27.880
So there are people
who do write ptx

00:55:27.880 --> 00:55:33.850
if you really think you're
better than the compiler.

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:38.630
And I think the NVIDIA compilers
are generally pretty mature,

00:55:38.630 --> 00:55:42.110
but some other accelerators
that are less developed.

00:55:42.110 --> 00:55:44.210
I think sometimes you
just have to reach in

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:47.650
and actually
handhold a bit more.

00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:51.870
But generally, yeah, you
shouldn't need to do that.

00:55:51.870 --> 00:55:52.490
Yeah?

00:55:52.490 --> 00:55:55.635
[INAUDIBLE]

00:55:56.530 --> 00:55:59.050
My work will get
scheduled onto an SM.

00:55:59.050 --> 00:56:01.250
I'll get to that
tf.load and it's almost

00:56:01.250 --> 00:56:03.810
like a CPU trap call
where I'm just waiting

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:07.430
for something to happen.

00:56:07.430 --> 00:56:10.330
And now I am turning my
thumbs and some other work

00:56:10.330 --> 00:56:12.430
will get scheduled over me.

00:56:12.430 --> 00:56:14.250
And then it will do operations.

00:56:14.250 --> 00:56:15.850
Now that you have
to upload is done,

00:56:15.850 --> 00:56:20.150
then I'll reschedule that work
and then I'll continue on.

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:21.170
Is that--

00:56:21.170 --> 00:56:22.350
Yeah, that's right.

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:27.370
So just to repeat the
question or the comment.

00:56:27.370 --> 00:56:30.700
So if you look at
this triangle code,

00:56:30.700 --> 00:56:34.300
these statements,
this load, is going

00:56:34.300 --> 00:56:36.700
to block for some
number of cycles.

00:56:36.700 --> 00:56:38.760
And then so this is
running on some thread

00:56:38.760 --> 00:56:40.500
on some warp on some SM.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:43.920
And remember, the SM is running
multiple warps at the same time.

00:56:43.920 --> 00:56:47.340
So when you get
to that point, it

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:50.583
can just find
another warp to run.

00:56:50.583 --> 00:56:52.500
And then when this is
done, the warp scheduler

00:56:52.500 --> 00:56:55.780
comes back and takes over.

00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:57.520
Why do you [INAUDIBLE]?

00:56:59.060 --> 00:56:59.900
Sorry.

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:01.140
Oh, sorry.

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:03.020
I recall from [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:06.620 --> 00:57:09.800
Yeah, so the question is,
why four warp schedules?

00:57:09.800 --> 00:57:12.760
I don't exactly the
reason behind that.

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:19.200
OK, so now let's go through
some other examples.

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:21.680
Maybe just as a quick preview.

00:57:21.680 --> 00:57:23.860
We're going to do
three more examples.

00:57:23.860 --> 00:57:28.060
GeLU is the simplest form.

00:57:28.060 --> 00:57:32.620
Even though the computation
has a lot of messiness.

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:33.740
It's just element wise.

00:57:33.740 --> 00:57:38.120
So conceptually, in the
context of this lecture,

00:57:38.120 --> 00:57:40.120
is actually very simple.

00:57:40.120 --> 00:57:44.280
Now we're going to look at
Softmax, where now you're

00:57:44.280 --> 00:57:46.312
going to have to do reduction.

00:57:46.312 --> 00:57:48.520
But in this case, we're
going to think about the case

00:57:48.520 --> 00:57:50.160
where row fits on a block.

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:53.130
And then we're going to
consider the case where

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:54.880
row doesn't fit on a
block, and then we're

00:57:54.880 --> 00:57:57.280
going to do go up to matmul.

00:57:57.280 --> 00:57:59.173
And then hopefully
by that point,

00:57:59.173 --> 00:58:00.840
you'll have all the
ingredients that you

00:58:00.840 --> 00:58:04.960
need to do the assignment and
implement flash attention.

00:58:04.960 --> 00:58:06.200
OK.

00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:10.680
So so far, we looked at
element wise operations.

00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:12.880
Now let's think about
other operations

00:58:12.880 --> 00:58:16.200
that aggregate over
multiple values.

00:58:16.200 --> 00:58:18.180
So remember what
the Softmax does.

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:23.720
Let's just think of as a matrix.

00:58:23.720 --> 00:58:29.210
You exponentiate and normalize
each row of a matrix.

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:33.890
OK, so this is used in intention
and used in generating output

00:58:33.890 --> 00:58:35.410
probabilities.

00:58:35.410 --> 00:58:39.410
Generally a good thing to do.

00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:43.890
So let's just start with
a naive implementation

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:46.550
and just keep track
of what's happening.

00:58:46.550 --> 00:58:49.650
So here, I'm
defining this tensor.

00:58:49.650 --> 00:58:55.370
And here's the naive
implementation.

00:58:55.370 --> 00:58:59.210
Actually, I think this
is on assignment one.

00:58:59.210 --> 00:59:00.550
OK, anyway.

00:59:00.550 --> 00:59:04.530
So here, I have
an M by N matrix.

00:59:04.530 --> 00:59:10.690
And so I need to, for
every row, compute the--

00:59:10.690 --> 00:59:13.530
I'm going to compute
the max of each row.

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:15.570
And this is for
numerical stability.

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:19.250
I'm going to
subtract off the max.

00:59:19.250 --> 00:59:23.590
And then I'm going to
exponentiate element-wise.

00:59:23.590 --> 00:59:27.300
And I'm going to sum and
compute the normalization

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:31.380
constant for every row.

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:34.900
And I'm going to divide.

00:59:34.900 --> 00:59:38.660
And then that's it.

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:40.660
OK.

00:59:40.660 --> 00:59:45.460
So if you count the number of
reads and writes, remember,

00:59:45.460 --> 00:59:47.180
this is just plain PyTorch.

00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:48.520
So this is a different kernel.

00:59:48.520 --> 00:59:49.700
This is a different kernel.

00:59:49.700 --> 00:59:51.900
And unless you call
to torch and compile,

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:54.660
these are going to be
different operations.

00:59:54.660 --> 00:59:57.580
And each operation is going
to read and write, read

00:59:57.580 --> 00:59:59.100
and write from HBM.

00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:06.660
OK, so you have five MN
reads, three MN writes.

01:00:06.660 --> 01:00:13.992
And in principle, you should
really only have much fewer.

01:00:13.992 --> 01:00:17.340
OK, so this is a piece of code.

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:18.080
Makes sense.

01:00:18.080 --> 01:00:21.140
Everyone should be familiar
what the Softmax is doing.

01:00:21.140 --> 01:00:23.350
OK.

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:28.230
So let's write down
the Triton kernel.

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:34.030
And in some sense, the form
is going to be very similar.

01:00:34.030 --> 01:00:35.470
Just like in the GeLU.

01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:37.030
Right, once you do
the scaffolding,

01:00:37.030 --> 01:00:42.430
the core computation looks very
much like the naive version.

01:00:42.430 --> 01:00:44.590
So what we're going
to do is we're going

01:00:44.590 --> 01:00:47.910
to say each row is a block.

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:48.590
OK.

01:00:48.590 --> 01:00:51.110
And why do I make
each row a block?

01:00:51.110 --> 01:00:53.310
Well, because now,
remember, each row,

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:55.010
I have to normalize and sum.

01:00:55.010 --> 01:00:56.490
So it's not element-wise.

01:00:56.490 --> 01:00:58.310
So Softmax is not
an element wise.

01:00:58.310 --> 01:01:00.390
But it is row wise.

01:01:00.390 --> 01:01:03.470
So the blocks don't interact.

01:01:03.470 --> 01:01:08.150
And the blocks don't have
shared memory, so that's fine.

01:01:08.150 --> 01:01:11.030
There's no shared
memory across blocks.

01:01:11.030 --> 01:01:16.590
So then within each row, I'm
just going to do some stuff.

01:01:16.590 --> 01:01:19.750
OK, so let's see what happens.

01:01:19.750 --> 01:01:22.230
So again, I'm going
to, in Python,

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:24.810
I'm going to allocate
my output tensor.

01:01:24.810 --> 01:01:28.890
I have this M by N matrix.

01:01:28.890 --> 01:01:35.210
I'm going to define the
blocksize as basically

01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:37.490
the number of columns.

01:01:37.490 --> 01:01:41.050
Go to the next power
of 2 for good luck.

01:01:41.050 --> 01:01:45.850
And then the number of blocks
is just the number of rows.

01:01:45.850 --> 01:01:48.730
OK, and I'm going
to call this kernel.

01:01:48.730 --> 01:01:51.876
So how many blocks are there?

01:01:51.876 --> 01:01:52.376
M.

01:01:52.376 --> 01:01:54.530
M. Right, one for each row.

01:01:54.530 --> 01:01:57.930
And each block, I'm going
to pass the input pointer,

01:01:57.930 --> 01:01:59.890
the output pointer.

01:01:59.890 --> 01:02:06.210
And then I'm going to also
pass these strides which

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:09.210
tell me how far to move down.

01:02:14.210 --> 01:02:20.940
OK, let me actually go and show
you that Softmax kernel, OK.

01:02:23.860 --> 01:02:28.860
OK, so what does
this look like here?

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:31.140
So I wake up.

01:02:31.140 --> 01:02:33.940
I'm on a particular row, OK.

01:02:33.940 --> 01:02:38.180
And this is going to give
me all the columns from 0

01:02:38.180 --> 01:02:41.060
to the number of blocksize,
which I'm assuming

01:02:41.060 --> 01:02:43.860
that's all the columns.

01:02:43.860 --> 01:02:48.820
I'm going to read.

01:02:48.820 --> 01:02:53.200
So basically, I need to figure
out where to read from memory,

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:55.780
and that's going to be
the start of my data.

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:59.060
Plus which row.

01:02:59.060 --> 01:03:01.460
And the row stride
basically gives me

01:03:01.460 --> 01:03:06.660
every row is-- basically,
this is the number of columns,

01:03:06.660 --> 01:03:07.500
essentially.

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:11.220
It's going to tell me
where how far to go down.

01:03:11.220 --> 01:03:14.020
And then this is x pointers
is basically the addresses

01:03:14.020 --> 01:03:15.660
of all the data I need to load.

01:03:15.660 --> 01:03:18.670
I load them up.

01:03:18.670 --> 01:03:23.190
And here, I do this thing
where if it's masked out, then

01:03:23.190 --> 01:03:26.990
I put minus infinity,
because that's

01:03:26.990 --> 01:03:33.110
going to be the equivalent of
a 0 for the Softmax operation.

01:03:33.110 --> 01:03:35.310
And then this part is
essentially the same

01:03:35.310 --> 01:03:37.210
as the naive Softmax.

01:03:37.210 --> 01:03:39.690
I'm just going to
compute the max,

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:44.070
subtract it off,
exponentiate, sum, and divide.

01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:45.330
And then I write it back.

01:03:48.950 --> 01:03:50.230
OK.

01:03:50.230 --> 01:03:51.110
Yeah.

01:03:51.110 --> 01:03:56.123
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:58.590 --> 01:04:03.310
Yeah, so this is the version
where each block can just

01:04:03.310 --> 01:04:07.030
span the entire row.

01:04:07.030 --> 01:04:13.090
And you can see that Triton
makes this very easy, right.

01:04:13.090 --> 01:04:16.650
Because this is as if you
were just writing normal

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:17.710
PyTorch code.

01:04:20.570 --> 01:04:23.630
So if everything fits in
a block, you basically--

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:25.990
the thing is, if you
can fit it into a block,

01:04:25.990 --> 01:04:29.330
you can just write
normal PyTorch, almost.

01:04:29.330 --> 01:04:30.490
Yeah?

01:04:30.490 --> 01:04:34.290
What if my number of
columns, the number of rows,

01:04:34.290 --> 01:04:37.865
are bigger than blocksize?

01:04:37.865 --> 01:04:38.990
Yeah, so we'll get to that.

01:04:38.990 --> 01:04:41.150
Now the number of
columns, rows, in general,

01:04:41.150 --> 01:04:43.150
it's going to be much
larger than the blocksize.

01:04:43.150 --> 01:04:44.525
So we'll come back
to that, yeah.

01:04:44.525 --> 01:04:47.610
So if you wanted to do
a Softmax per column,

01:04:47.610 --> 01:04:48.990
do you have to do [INAUDIBLE]?

01:04:52.530 --> 01:04:56.490
So the question is if you
want to do Softmax by column.

01:04:56.490 --> 01:04:59.890
I think that should be
fine, because here, we're

01:04:59.890 --> 01:05:01.350
tracking these pointers, right.

01:05:01.350 --> 01:05:05.258
So the pointers can be anything.

01:05:05.258 --> 01:05:07.050
Basically, I think all
you would have to do

01:05:07.050 --> 01:05:12.265
is change the stride here to
basically access the columns.

01:05:12.265 --> 01:05:13.390
Actually, it would be here.

01:05:13.390 --> 01:05:18.060
The column offsets, you
would just multiply this

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:21.620
by the row stride, I think.

01:05:21.620 --> 01:05:23.780
OK, let's move on.

01:05:23.780 --> 01:05:30.500
All right, so now,
warming up to the matmul.

01:05:30.500 --> 01:05:34.060
Suppose that your row
doesn't fit into a block.

01:05:34.060 --> 01:05:36.700
OK, so what do you
do in this case?

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:39.420
So for example, if
you have, let's say,

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:46.420
a row that's 4,000 columns, but
the blocksize is only 1,024.

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:50.660
So you have to do
something here.

01:05:50.660 --> 01:05:52.700
So here's the strategy.

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:56.060
We're going to break
up the row into tiles.

01:05:56.060 --> 01:05:58.860
So in this case, there's
going to be four tiles.

01:05:58.860 --> 01:06:02.780
And each thread is going
to iterate over the tiles

01:06:02.780 --> 01:06:04.860
and accumulate a sum.

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:07.260
And then finally
at the end, we're

01:06:07.260 --> 01:06:15.910
going to do the reduction
by summing everything

01:06:15.910 --> 01:06:17.530
that each thread produced.

01:06:17.530 --> 01:06:19.770
So I'll show you
an example of this.

01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:21.990
And now I'm switching
from Softmax

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:27.050
to row sum because it's
just easier to think about.

01:06:27.050 --> 01:06:32.770
OK, so here, OK, well, this
is not very interesting.

01:06:32.770 --> 01:06:37.350
The built-in row sum does
what you would expect.

01:06:37.350 --> 01:06:42.470
So the row sum operation
just basically takes a matrix

01:06:42.470 --> 01:06:45.310
and then computes the
sum of each row, OK.

01:06:45.310 --> 01:06:48.630
So just no surprises there.

01:06:48.630 --> 01:06:53.470
OK, so conceptually what we're
going to do here is as follows.

01:06:53.470 --> 01:06:57.030
OK, so each block is still
in charge of one row.

01:06:57.030 --> 01:06:58.950
So that part hasn't changed.

01:06:58.950 --> 01:07:01.750
So suppose block one, row one.

01:07:01.750 --> 01:07:03.790
I wake up, and what do I do?

01:07:03.790 --> 01:07:05.890
I'm going to remember,
now, there's tiles.

01:07:05.890 --> 01:07:08.930
And suppose tile 0 is
columns 0 through 3,

01:07:08.930 --> 01:07:12.060
tile 1 is columns 4 through 7.

01:07:12.060 --> 01:07:15.720
And then tile 2 is
columns 8 through 11.

01:07:15.720 --> 01:07:18.440
OK, so what I'm
going to do here is

01:07:18.440 --> 01:07:20.980
that I'm going to iterate now.

01:07:20.980 --> 01:07:26.500
So first, I basically process
this, all the threads.

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:29.040
Basically, each thread
keeps a kind of accumulator

01:07:29.040 --> 01:07:32.040
and processes the first tile.

01:07:32.040 --> 01:07:34.520
And it's going to move
on to the next tile,

01:07:34.520 --> 01:07:38.360
and it's going to add
the current element

01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:39.420
to the accumulator.

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:43.040
So here, I'm putting 3, 1, 4, 1.

01:07:43.040 --> 01:07:47.700
And then the second
loop iteration,

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:49.240
I'm going to add 5 to this.

01:07:49.240 --> 01:07:50.120
I get 8.

01:07:50.120 --> 01:07:51.920
I add 9 to this, I get 10.

01:07:51.920 --> 01:07:54.460
2 to 4, and 6 to 1, right.

01:07:54.460 --> 01:07:56.360
So each of these
four threads is going

01:07:56.360 --> 01:08:00.160
to be accumulating
its own thing.

01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:03.920
And then at the end, I
do another for tile 2.

01:08:03.920 --> 01:08:08.520
I add 5 and 3 to the
respective accumulators.

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:13.800
So at the end of the day, I have
this, a vector of accumulators.

01:08:13.800 --> 01:08:17.260
And then that is actually--

01:08:17.260 --> 01:08:21.000
that, I can just sum up.

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:25.500
OK, so let's see what
the code looks like here.

01:08:25.500 --> 01:08:28.899
So I'm just going to
call the row sum kernel.

01:08:28.899 --> 01:08:32.913
And let's see.

01:08:32.913 --> 01:08:35.700
OK, so here's what
it looks like.

01:08:35.700 --> 01:08:36.540
So wake up.

01:08:36.540 --> 01:08:38.660
I am on a particular row.

01:08:38.660 --> 01:08:42.819
And this is what
one row looks like.

01:08:42.819 --> 01:08:46.399
There's one tile, a second
tile, a third tile, and so on.

01:08:46.399 --> 01:08:50.660
So n is the number of
elements of that row.

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:58.340
And blocksize is the
size of this tile.

01:08:58.340 --> 01:08:59.260
OK.

01:08:59.260 --> 01:09:01.500
So remember, blocksize
is the number of threads.

01:09:01.500 --> 01:09:04.880
I'm processing more data than
the larger than the blocksize.

01:09:04.880 --> 01:09:07.340
I just have to do
it iteratively now.

01:09:07.340 --> 01:09:10.790
So I'm going to loop
over all of the tiles.

01:09:10.790 --> 01:09:14.630
So start is going to
go from 0 to blocksize

01:09:14.630 --> 01:09:18.310
to 2 times blocksize,
and so on and so forth.

01:09:18.310 --> 01:09:26.830
So each time I'm jumping
across, I am doing calls.

01:09:26.830 --> 01:09:35.750
Basically get the offsets
at the particular tile.

01:09:35.750 --> 01:09:40.149
And then I'm going to
load the data from HBM,

01:09:40.149 --> 01:09:42.609
and I'm going to add
it to the accumulation.

01:09:42.609 --> 01:09:46.350
So this accumulation is going to
be either in registers or shared

01:09:46.350 --> 01:09:48.069
memory.

01:09:48.069 --> 01:09:49.308
OK.

01:09:49.308 --> 01:09:51.350
And then finally after I
loop over all the tiles,

01:09:51.350 --> 01:09:54.189
I process the whole
row, I get basically--

01:09:54.189 --> 01:09:57.270
for every thread I have
accumulator of what

01:09:57.270 --> 01:09:59.170
that thread has picked up.

01:09:59.170 --> 01:10:03.290
And I can just do a sum to get
a scalar, and I write it out.

01:10:05.865 --> 01:10:08.240
OK, so this is a little bit
more complicated than before,

01:10:08.240 --> 01:10:10.560
because now we have a
for loop within a thread.

01:10:10.560 --> 01:10:15.260
And this is necessary when your
data doesn't fit within a block.

01:10:18.320 --> 01:10:19.220
Yeah?

01:10:19.220 --> 01:10:22.013
[INAUDIBLE]

01:10:25.520 --> 01:10:29.140
Just in case we don't
have enough register.

01:10:29.140 --> 01:10:30.600
We have to put it in--

01:10:30.600 --> 01:10:33.120
Yeah, so the question is
I think if you can control

01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:35.320
where the accumulator resides.

01:10:35.320 --> 01:10:39.520
At least in this Triton program,
you don't explicitly say.

01:10:39.520 --> 01:10:42.560
And this is up to
the Triton compiler

01:10:42.560 --> 01:10:45.340
to figure out where to put it.

01:10:47.978 --> 01:10:50.020
But in general, if the
blocksize is large enough,

01:10:50.020 --> 01:10:52.660
it has to go in shared memory.

01:10:55.480 --> 01:10:56.980
OK.

01:10:56.980 --> 01:10:59.540
All right, does this make sense?

01:11:02.320 --> 01:11:06.320
Just to, I think, make sure
people are on the same page.

01:11:06.320 --> 01:11:09.340
Remember when GeLU,
we also split a row

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:11.660
into a bunch of pieces.

01:11:11.660 --> 01:11:13.580
But those were blocks.

01:11:13.580 --> 01:11:15.260
Right, and so each
of those pieces

01:11:15.260 --> 01:11:18.060
was a block that was
processed independently.

01:11:18.060 --> 01:11:19.120
These are not blocks.

01:11:19.120 --> 01:11:21.220
These are tiles.

01:11:21.220 --> 01:11:24.820
The block corresponds
to this whole row.

01:11:24.820 --> 01:11:28.560
And the block has to basically
process all the tiles.

01:11:28.560 --> 01:11:32.780
And so this is where it starts
to not look like PyTorch,

01:11:32.780 --> 01:11:35.460
because you're not
able to process all

01:11:35.460 --> 01:11:37.740
your data in one nice kind of--

01:11:37.740 --> 01:11:40.060
not everything fits
into shared memory.

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:40.820
Yeah?

01:11:40.820 --> 01:11:42.420
[INAUDIBLE]

01:11:42.420 --> 01:11:44.460
--also stored in shared memory?

01:11:44.460 --> 01:11:45.580
Yeah.

01:11:45.580 --> 01:11:48.120
Let's say the accumulator
is stored in shared memory.

01:11:48.120 --> 01:11:48.620
Yeah.

01:11:51.460 --> 01:11:53.380
OK.

01:11:53.380 --> 01:11:57.620
All right, so let's
now maybe go on

01:11:57.620 --> 01:12:00.720
to our finale, which is matmul.

01:12:00.720 --> 01:12:03.870
OK, so matmul, of course,
is the bread and butter

01:12:03.870 --> 01:12:04.930
of deep learning.

01:12:04.930 --> 01:12:07.750
It's been optimized to death.

01:12:07.750 --> 01:12:14.330
And it's, in some sense,
very fundamental operation.

01:12:14.330 --> 01:12:16.530
So you take two matrices,
you multiply them.

01:12:16.530 --> 01:12:19.270
I'm going to add a little
bit of a twist here.

01:12:19.270 --> 01:12:21.950
I'm going to do a matmul
followed by a ReLU,

01:12:21.950 --> 01:12:24.190
just because, just for kicks.

01:12:24.190 --> 01:12:26.510
OK.

01:12:26.510 --> 01:12:28.010
Well, this happens, right.

01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:31.410
Because if you have one
linear layer, it's a matmul.

01:12:31.410 --> 01:12:34.490
And then you apply
a ReLU activation.

01:12:34.490 --> 01:12:37.750
So this is not completely
out of nowhere.

01:12:37.750 --> 01:12:41.350
But I'll show you
later why I did this.

01:12:41.350 --> 01:12:44.510
OK, so how do you
build a matmul kernel?

01:12:44.510 --> 01:12:48.630
All right, so here's
a naive approach.

01:12:48.630 --> 01:12:51.070
OK, so here's my,
let's say, a matrix.

01:12:51.070 --> 01:12:54.950
I'm multiplying A
times B. And I'm

01:12:54.950 --> 01:13:01.330
trying to write out the matrix
C. And A is M by K, B is K by N.

01:13:01.330 --> 01:13:05.400
So C is M by N. OK, so
what I'm going to do

01:13:05.400 --> 01:13:09.700
is I'm going to fix
one of these elements.

01:13:09.700 --> 01:13:12.340
Let's say M equals
1, N equals 2.

01:13:12.340 --> 01:13:16.200
OK, so I'm processing--
actually, let's do M equals 1,

01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:16.820
N equals 1.

01:13:16.820 --> 01:13:18.560
So I'm doing C5.

01:13:18.560 --> 01:13:28.120
And then, basically for every
k, so I'm going to, yeah,

01:13:28.120 --> 01:13:32.920
I'm going to iterate
over the k, the rows of A

01:13:32.920 --> 01:13:37.040
and the columns of B. I'm
going to read from HBM.

01:13:37.040 --> 01:13:39.580
I'm going to multiply
them, accumulate that.

01:13:39.580 --> 01:13:41.440
And at the end, I'm
going to write out

01:13:41.440 --> 01:13:44.280
to this and this element.

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:47.880
So that is a valid
matmul kernel.

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:49.200
OK.

01:13:49.200 --> 01:13:51.300
So what's wrong with it?

01:13:57.720 --> 01:13:58.760
It's correct.

01:13:58.760 --> 01:14:03.170
But if you look at how many
reads and writes it's doing,

01:14:03.170 --> 01:14:04.490
this is not good.

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:08.530
Right, so basically for
every M and N and K,

01:14:08.530 --> 01:14:10.230
I have to read from HBM.

01:14:10.230 --> 01:14:14.002
So it's on the order of
M times K times N reads.

01:14:14.002 --> 01:14:14.710
Number of writes.

01:14:14.710 --> 01:14:18.650
I mean, it doesn't matter, but
this is the bottleneck, right.

01:14:18.650 --> 01:14:22.850
And if you remember
from the second lecture,

01:14:22.850 --> 01:14:25.410
if you look at the
number of operations

01:14:25.410 --> 01:14:27.730
you're doing divided by
the number of bytes that

01:14:27.730 --> 01:14:30.250
were transferred, that's
the arithmetic intensity

01:14:30.250 --> 01:14:32.210
which you want to be high.

01:14:32.210 --> 01:14:36.890
So the number of operations
is M times K times N order.

01:14:36.890 --> 01:14:38.870
And then the number of
reads is also the same.

01:14:38.870 --> 01:14:43.530
So arithmetic intensity is a
constant, which is not good.

01:14:43.530 --> 01:14:47.210
OK, so if you look
carefully, you

01:14:47.210 --> 01:14:49.310
notice that there is a
lot of redundant reads.

01:14:49.310 --> 01:14:52.810
So imagine computing C4.

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:56.450
You needed to read
A4, A5, and A6.

01:14:56.450 --> 01:14:58.650
And if you compute
C5, you're going

01:14:58.650 --> 01:15:02.030
to have to read those
over again as well.

01:15:02.030 --> 01:15:07.270
So if you can just
read those once,

01:15:07.270 --> 01:15:10.170
then you really save on
reads, and that's great.

01:15:10.170 --> 01:15:14.670
So let's try to use
shared memory to do that.

01:15:14.670 --> 01:15:17.950
So here's the
idealized approach.

01:15:17.950 --> 01:15:21.430
I'm going to load all of A
and B into shared memory.

01:15:21.430 --> 01:15:25.390
And then I'm going
to just compute C. So

01:15:25.390 --> 01:15:28.510
if I can do that, then I get--

01:15:28.510 --> 01:15:30.450
now I don't have this
cubic number of reads,

01:15:30.450 --> 01:15:32.870
I only get quadratic
number of reads,

01:15:32.870 --> 01:15:37.550
which means I get the error
intensity of order n, which

01:15:37.550 --> 01:15:42.270
in the second lecture, I said
was a kind of ideal thing

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:45.550
you could hope for.

01:15:45.550 --> 01:15:47.990
OK, so if you can
do that, that's

01:15:47.990 --> 01:15:50.670
great, because you basically--
there's no redundant reads.

01:15:50.670 --> 01:15:52.650
You read everything
once into shared memory.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:55.510
You do the computation
and you write it back.

01:15:55.510 --> 01:15:58.380
OK, but what's the problem
with this idealized approach.

01:16:03.280 --> 01:16:05.520
The problem is that
A and B are usually

01:16:05.520 --> 01:16:09.166
too large to fit
into shared memory.

01:16:09.166 --> 01:16:10.440
Right.

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:14.280
So then, what do
you have to do now?

01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:19.640
OK, so the idea here is this
very classic idea of tiling.

01:16:19.640 --> 01:16:23.520
And the idea basically
is to, well, fit as much

01:16:23.520 --> 01:16:26.680
as you can to shared memory
as you can, essentially.

01:16:26.680 --> 01:16:29.440
So in some sense,
it's going to look

01:16:29.440 --> 01:16:32.585
like the naive-- it's going to
globally look like the naive

01:16:32.585 --> 01:16:34.960
approach, but locally it look
like the idealized approach

01:16:34.960 --> 01:16:38.320
is the way to think about it.

01:16:38.320 --> 01:16:41.480
So here's the picture.

01:16:41.480 --> 01:16:43.463
I think Tatsu
showed this as well.

01:16:43.463 --> 01:16:44.880
So what we're going
to do is we're

01:16:44.880 --> 01:16:48.240
going to take this matrix
C, and instead-- remember,

01:16:48.240 --> 01:16:50.400
the naive approach just
said for every element,

01:16:50.400 --> 01:16:52.180
I'm going to compute it.

01:16:52.180 --> 01:16:55.990
But now I'm going to say for
every tile, I'm going to do it.

01:16:55.990 --> 01:16:58.890
So I break it up into tiles.

01:16:58.890 --> 01:17:02.610
And each of these tiles is
going to be a thread block.

01:17:02.610 --> 01:17:04.610
And I'm going to have a
bunch of threads that is

01:17:04.610 --> 01:17:07.450
responsible for computing this.

01:17:07.450 --> 01:17:09.410
Now a different
tile is going to be

01:17:09.410 --> 01:17:14.090
computed completely separately
by another thread block.

01:17:14.090 --> 01:17:16.290
OK, so I'm going to--

01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:17.910
imagine I'm in Triton.

01:17:17.910 --> 01:17:19.850
I wake up.

01:17:19.850 --> 01:17:21.690
I'm looking at
this thread block.

01:17:21.690 --> 01:17:23.290
So what do I have to do?

01:17:23.290 --> 01:17:24.730
Well, in some
sense, it's the same

01:17:24.730 --> 01:17:32.450
as the naive approach where,
for every row tile of A,

01:17:32.450 --> 01:17:35.010
I'm going to go scan
across the rows.

01:17:35.010 --> 01:17:38.210
And for every column
tile of B, I'm

01:17:38.210 --> 01:17:43.250
going to load the corresponding
tile A and the corresponding--

01:17:43.250 --> 01:17:45.850
so say I'm here-- and
the corresponding tile

01:17:45.850 --> 01:17:48.650
from B into shared memory.

01:17:48.650 --> 01:17:51.450
I'm going to multiply
these two together.

01:17:51.450 --> 01:17:58.400
And that's going to be like
the idealized approach.

01:17:58.400 --> 01:18:02.400
And I'm going to accumulate
that in the partial sum.

01:18:02.400 --> 01:18:04.140
And this is all sitting
in shared memory.

01:18:04.140 --> 01:18:07.660
And then after I finish all the
sweep of the row and the column

01:18:07.660 --> 01:18:11.080
here, then I can finally
write this output tile to HBM.

01:18:15.340 --> 01:18:20.380
OK, so that's the
conceptually what's happening.

01:18:20.380 --> 01:18:22.180
And then the arithmetic
intensity here

01:18:22.180 --> 01:18:24.900
now goes up to order tile size.

01:18:24.900 --> 01:18:27.700
So you can generally
not reach order n,

01:18:27.700 --> 01:18:29.940
because that would require
you to fit everything

01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:31.120
into shared memory.

01:18:31.120 --> 01:18:36.860
But if your tiles are big,
then that's still not too bad.

01:18:36.860 --> 01:18:39.100
OK, so just as a bonus.

01:18:39.100 --> 01:18:43.260
While you're doing all
writing a kernel for this

01:18:43.260 --> 01:18:47.300
anyway, sometimes if you want to
apply an element-wise activation

01:18:47.300 --> 01:18:51.500
function, it's very easy to
just put it on at the end.

01:18:51.500 --> 01:18:53.540
And this is kernel fusion.

01:18:53.540 --> 01:18:58.680
OK, so very quickly,
the implementation here.

01:18:58.680 --> 01:19:03.720
So-- oops.

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:07.960
OK, so just a reminder
about strides since this

01:19:07.960 --> 01:19:09.120
is going to show up.

01:19:09.120 --> 01:19:14.200
So a tensor is a
multidimensional array.

01:19:14.200 --> 01:19:16.800
But in memory, it's linearized.

01:19:16.800 --> 01:19:19.640
And strides of a tensor
tells you basically

01:19:19.640 --> 01:19:22.400
how to map from a
multidimensional index

01:19:22.400 --> 01:19:26.900
such as a row, a column,
into an actual index.

01:19:26.900 --> 01:19:28.840
And basically what you
do is multiply the row

01:19:28.840 --> 01:19:30.960
by the stride, the
plus the column

01:19:30.960 --> 01:19:33.320
by the stride of the column.

01:19:33.320 --> 01:19:37.220
OK, so in this case, every time
you go advance to the next row,

01:19:37.220 --> 01:19:40.260
you go four positions
in your memory.

01:19:40.260 --> 01:19:42.600
And every time you advance
a column, you go one.

01:19:42.600 --> 01:19:46.560
And if it were to transpose,
it would be flipped.

01:19:46.560 --> 01:19:50.540
OK, so what does this
kernel look like here?

01:19:54.810 --> 01:19:57.610
OK, so the launch
is not interesting.

01:19:57.610 --> 01:20:02.350
So you wake up and you
are on tile M and N.

01:20:02.350 --> 01:20:07.590
So it's like OK, I'm responsible
for computing the c matrix,

01:20:07.590 --> 01:20:11.930
but for the M comma
N tile there's

01:20:11.930 --> 01:20:15.410
a bunch of index
manipulation which you can--

01:20:15.410 --> 01:20:19.690
I'll just gloss through,
but it's straightforward

01:20:19.690 --> 01:20:20.530
but a little bit.

01:20:20.530 --> 01:20:24.610
You just have to
track the indices.

01:20:24.610 --> 01:20:29.530
So this basically tells you
which rows of A, the matrix A,

01:20:29.530 --> 01:20:30.570
I'm looking at.

01:20:30.570 --> 01:20:34.310
Which rows of-- oh sorry, which
columns of B I'm looking at.

01:20:34.310 --> 01:20:38.370
And then this is just
the numbers 1 through K.

01:20:38.370 --> 01:20:42.970
Then I'm going to get
the pointers into A and B

01:20:42.970 --> 01:20:46.330
at my tile location.

01:20:46.330 --> 01:20:48.750
And then I'm going to set
up this accumulator matrix.

01:20:48.750 --> 01:20:51.500
This is going to be
in shared memory.

01:20:51.500 --> 01:20:53.660
It's M by N. And
then this is going

01:20:53.660 --> 01:20:57.320
to look like the row
reduction, right.

01:20:57.320 --> 01:21:01.780
You have this sum
over all the tiles.

01:21:01.780 --> 01:21:04.180
But instead of just
going across the tiles,

01:21:04.180 --> 01:21:06.860
I'm now going
across the row tiles

01:21:06.860 --> 01:21:08.860
and also simultaneously
down the column

01:21:08.860 --> 01:21:13.820
tiles of B. I load A, the small
matrix, I load B, the small B

01:21:13.820 --> 01:21:14.540
tile.

01:21:14.540 --> 01:21:16.397
And then I perform this dot.

01:21:16.397 --> 01:21:18.480
So remember, whenever
things are in shared memory,

01:21:18.480 --> 01:21:21.220
things look like PyTorch and
I can just say, matmul it,

01:21:21.220 --> 01:21:23.940
and it will do the thing.

01:21:23.940 --> 01:21:29.300
And then I advance to
the next row tile of A

01:21:29.300 --> 01:21:34.540
and the next column tile of B.

01:21:34.540 --> 01:21:37.620
The bonus is that
if I wanted to apply

01:21:37.620 --> 01:21:42.580
element-wise non-linearity,
I might as well do that here.

01:21:42.580 --> 01:21:47.480
Before I write it out to HBM, I
can do some any operation on it.

01:21:47.480 --> 01:21:51.120
And then finally, I
just write it out.

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:55.060
OK, so there's some indices that
you have to pay attention to.

01:21:55.060 --> 01:21:59.540
But hopefully, the form
of the algorithm is clear.

01:22:06.260 --> 01:22:06.760
OK.

01:22:06.760 --> 01:22:09.020
Any questions about that?

01:22:12.200 --> 01:22:12.700
All right.

01:22:12.700 --> 01:22:15.100
Maybe I'll just summarize,
and you can ask me later.

01:22:15.100 --> 01:22:18.360
So today, we talked
about there's

01:22:18.360 --> 01:22:20.520
a programming model,
which is you're

01:22:20.520 --> 01:22:25.720
talking about either
PyTorch or Triton or ptx.

01:22:25.720 --> 01:22:32.000
This is what the kind of
programmer can control.

01:22:32.000 --> 01:22:33.160
Right, and even ptx.

01:22:33.160 --> 01:22:36.600
You can write ptx and you can
control every-- specialize

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:38.560
however you want.

01:22:38.560 --> 01:22:40.900
But this is not
the full picture,

01:22:40.900 --> 01:22:45.480
because the reality is that your
code has to run on hardware,

01:22:45.480 --> 01:22:48.770
and there's only a
finite number of SMs,

01:22:48.770 --> 01:22:54.470
a finite number of banks, and
sizing of memory and registers.

01:22:54.470 --> 01:22:55.650
All are finite.

01:22:55.650 --> 01:22:58.370
So you come in with your
big matrix and transformer,

01:22:58.370 --> 01:23:01.730
and want to-- it has to
fit in with the constraints

01:23:01.730 --> 01:23:04.810
of the hardware.

01:23:04.810 --> 01:23:07.570
So that's why
benchmarking and profiling

01:23:07.570 --> 01:23:09.970
are really important
to understand

01:23:09.970 --> 01:23:13.050
how the messiness
of the hardware

01:23:13.050 --> 01:23:16.250
translates to performance.

01:23:16.250 --> 01:23:18.510
We talked about
Triton, which is,

01:23:18.510 --> 01:23:21.410
I think, a pretty nice,
neat language to think

01:23:21.410 --> 01:23:23.650
about thread blocks.

01:23:23.650 --> 01:23:25.610
Hopefully by now
you can appreciate

01:23:25.610 --> 01:23:30.730
that things are easier to
think about thread blocks

01:23:30.730 --> 01:23:33.170
than individual threads,
because you don't have

01:23:33.170 --> 01:23:37.170
to think about explicitly
synchronizing threads or doing

01:23:37.170 --> 01:23:38.490
shared memory.

01:23:38.490 --> 01:23:41.330
And the way to think about it
is that you figure out-- you

01:23:41.330 --> 01:23:42.310
have your computation.

01:23:42.310 --> 01:23:44.700
You break it down
into the thread

01:23:44.700 --> 01:23:47.200
blocks where you just need
to read from shared memory.

01:23:47.200 --> 01:23:50.380
Do some stuff, and
write it back into HBM.

01:23:50.380 --> 01:23:53.360
And then we saw some examples
of increasing difficulty.

01:23:53.360 --> 01:23:55.080
Element wise is the easiest.

01:23:55.080 --> 01:23:57.660
And then reduction over a row.

01:23:57.660 --> 01:24:00.760
Reduction where it
doesn't fit into a row.

01:24:00.760 --> 01:24:04.660
And we introduced
the baby tiling.

01:24:04.660 --> 01:24:08.540
And then matmul is
the canonical example

01:24:08.540 --> 01:24:10.660
where you actually do tiling.

01:24:10.660 --> 01:24:13.860
OK, so that's all I'm
going to say about how

01:24:13.860 --> 01:24:15.040
to program a single GPU.

01:24:15.040 --> 01:24:18.220
Next time, we're going to
go to more GPU and talk

01:24:18.220 --> 01:24:21.540
about multi-GPU programming.

01:24:21.540 --> 01:24:22.980
Yeah, question.

01:24:22.980 --> 01:24:24.717
So if I want to
write down kernels,

01:24:24.717 --> 01:24:25.800
what are the alternatives?

01:24:25.800 --> 01:24:27.860
Do I have to try them and--

01:24:27.860 --> 01:24:29.700
for each of my
choices that I have,

01:24:29.700 --> 01:24:33.300
how close can I get
to the optimal level?

01:24:33.300 --> 01:24:39.580
So the question is, what
are alternatives to Triton?

01:24:39.580 --> 01:24:42.870
So there's a trade off between--

01:24:42.870 --> 01:24:47.070
every language has an
inductive bias, right.

01:24:47.070 --> 01:24:50.310
That makes certain things easier
and certain things harder.

01:24:50.310 --> 01:24:51.870
So most of what we do--

01:24:51.870 --> 01:24:56.350
Triton was built by people
who train transformers.

01:24:56.350 --> 01:24:59.450
So anything involving
transformers,

01:24:59.450 --> 01:25:04.322
I think it's going to be
relatively easy there.

01:25:04.322 --> 01:25:06.030
Of course, in the
extreme, you can always

01:25:06.030 --> 01:25:08.090
go to ptx and write that.

01:25:08.090 --> 01:25:10.410
But I wouldn't advise
that as a first step.

01:25:10.410 --> 01:25:14.070
There are a bunch
of other libraries.

01:25:14.070 --> 01:25:15.770
There's thunder kittens.

01:25:15.770 --> 01:25:19.310
There's cute,
delirious DSLs that

01:25:19.310 --> 01:25:24.710
allow you to give you-- they're
not necessarily comparable

01:25:24.710 --> 01:25:26.050
either up or down the stack.

01:25:26.050 --> 01:25:30.630
They just give you
different characteristics.

01:25:30.630 --> 01:25:31.630
Yeah?

01:25:31.630 --> 01:25:35.170
Do you have a high dimensional
tensor magnification

01:25:35.170 --> 01:25:40.150
or high dimensional
tensor processing?

01:25:40.150 --> 01:25:42.570
What is the best approach?

01:25:42.570 --> 01:25:49.250
What I'm thinking is can I load
unload the whole sensor, both

01:25:49.250 --> 01:25:53.450
of the sensors on bunch of
threads or thread blocks

01:25:53.450 --> 01:25:57.330
on my GPU at the same
time and compute them?

01:25:57.330 --> 01:26:03.490
Or would it be better
to take each element,

01:26:03.490 --> 01:26:06.210
just what we did here, right.

01:26:06.210 --> 01:26:09.210
A particular component
of my sensor,

01:26:09.210 --> 01:26:12.730
then process it and then
write it back in the end.

01:26:12.730 --> 01:26:15.570
Yeah, so briefly, the question--
and we should probably wrap up--

01:26:15.570 --> 01:26:19.450
is, is it better
to read all at once

01:26:19.450 --> 01:26:24.388
or maybe process individual
elements at a time?

01:26:24.388 --> 01:26:26.430
I think it's hard to answer
this in the abstract.

01:26:26.430 --> 01:26:30.930
It depends on the nature
of the computation.

01:26:30.930 --> 01:26:33.490
Maybe we can talk
offline about that.

01:26:33.490 --> 01:26:34.630
OK, all right.

01:26:34.630 --> 01:26:36.600
See you next time.

